  "body": "## What changed\n\n#3874 asked the CLI to talk only to the real window. The first cut answered with a token-proof exchange; mkinney's review showed that exchange cannot work: the hello gives the token to the very peer it tries to check, so a false server that never read the discovery file still receives it and can compute the proof. This cut replaces it with a transport that verifies the peer.\n\n- **Windows**: the window listens on a named pipe `\\\\.\\pipe\\camcraft-ui-link-<pid>` with a security descriptor that admits the window's own user only. The CLI asks the kernel which process serves the pipe (`GetNamedPipeServerProcessId`) and refuses a peer that is not the process the discovery file names - before one byte of the token crosses. The nonce/proof exchange is removed. A `\"tcp\"` entry is refused by name on every platform, with the risk in the diagnostic; this build never dials one.\n- **Unix** keeps the 0700 socket folder; the review's read of the issue is that the risk does not occur there.\n- **One hello deadline**: the connect, the hello write and the answer read run under one elapsed clock; a peer that trickles bytes ends within it (exit 4).\n- **Bounded lines**: a line that reaches the 1 MiB bound without its newline closes the link; no remainder of an oversized line is parsed as a new message.\n- **Bounded bridge**: reaching the bridge's copy bound with the session still open is a visible failure (exit 1), never a quiet success after a truncated answer.\n\nThe decision record is `docs/decisions/3874-ui-link-transport.md`.\n\n## Dependency decision the maintainer must make\n\nThe PR declares two dependencies the maintainer has to confirm (both are already in `Cargo.lock` through other deps; no new crate):\n\n- `windows-sys` on Windows: the pid-alive check (`OpenProcess`) and the named-pipe transport itself (`CreateNamedPipeW`, `ConnectNamedPipe`, `GetNamedPipeServerProcessId`, the SDDL user-only descriptor). A named pipe needs it - std has no pipe API; the `interprocess` crate is not in the lock and would be a new-crate decision. Features: `Win32_Foundation`, `Win32_System_Threading`, `Win32_System_IO`, `Win32_System_Pipes`, `Win32_Storage_FileSystem`, `Win32_Security`, `Win32_Security_Authorization`.\n- `libc` on Unix: the pid-alive check through `kill(0)`.\n\n## Readings (final head)\n\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test mcp_live ui_link`: 11 passed - the false-pipe pin (a pipe served by another process receives nothing: no token, no tool call), the TCP-refused pin, the slow-peer deadline, the oversized line with and without a final newline, the bridge bound, the busy exit 4, the wrong token, the stale-file removal.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test mcp_live` (full binary): 25 passed.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --lib`: 307 passed.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The pipe transport on Unix (the pipe code is `cfg(windows)`); the Unix path keeps the socket transport and its tests.\n- A second local user is not emulated; the pipe's user-only ACL is kernel-enforced, and the CLI-side proof is the kernel-reported server process id.\n\nChangelog: `camcraft-cli --ui` talks to the window over a named pipe with a user-only ACL on Windows (a TCP link address is refused), under one hello deadline, with capped answer lines and a bridge bound that reports a truncated session.\n\nFixes #3874\n",
